WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.968
A sequence pair uses two rows of letters to encode every pair of blocks.

2
00:00:06.058 --> 00:00:11.994
A comes before B in both permutations, so A must be left of B.

3
00:00:13.084 --> 00:00:20.028
A precedes D in the first row but follows D in the second; here that means A is below D.

4
00:00:21.118 --> 00:00:29.046
D must clear every block below it, not only A, so take the maximum over all predecessor bounds.

5
00:00:30.136 --> 00:00:35.472
B’s top is one unit higher than A’s; predict the final height of D.

6
00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:43.964
The reference pair produces a nine-by-four box; it is a different encoding from the previous lesson’s reference tree.

7
00:00:45.054 --> 00:00:52.142
Make both permutations identical; the blocks line up without overlap but extend beyond the outline.

8
00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:59.216
Change the permutations in the lab, then implement the longest-path updates in Python.
